# Short report:
# Under the null, both tests reject close to 0.05 with equal variances (0.044
# Welch and 0.038 pooled), but with unequal variances the pooled test rejects
# far too often (0.139) while Welch stays near 0.05 (0.061), so pooled has an
# inflated Type I error rate. When the means differ, both tests have high
# power with equal variances (about 0.96), and with unequal variances pooled
# has higher power than Welch (0.497 vs 0.282). That extra power is not a
# real advantage, because a test must also control Type I error, and results
# vary by about +/- 0.014 around 0.05 with B = 1000.
